; A POKEY audio fixture.
;
; ORIGINAL WORK. Nothing here comes from the game source, and it is committable
; for the same reason `mob.MAC` is: it is a program we wrote, exercising
; hardware we modelled.
;
; # Why it exists
;
; The audio path was built by measuring the verilated core one behaviour at a
; time — the polynomials, the clock divisors, the distortion table. Each of
; those measurements looked at one signal in isolation. This programme runs the
; whole path at once and lets the two implementations be compared sample for
; sample, which is a different and stronger question: not "is each part right"
; but "do they together produce the same sound".
;
; # What it programs
;
; Both chips, so the board's `SOUT = snd1 + snd2` summation is exercised rather
; than assumed:
;
;   POKEY0 channel 1   pure tone, AUDF 5, volume 7
;   POKEY0 channel 2   volume-only, level 3 — a DC offset that must appear on
;                      every sample, so a channel silently dropping out shows
;   POKEY1 channel 1   pure tone, AUDF 11, volume 5
;
; The two tones are at different frequencies deliberately: summed, they beat,
; and the beat pattern is far more discriminating than either tone alone. A
; divider off by one would keep both tones square and still destroy the beat.
;
; # What it deliberately leaves out
;
; No channel selects a polynomial distortion. The polynomials themselves were
; measured and are tested against those measurements, but their *phase at
; reset* is not established — the fixtures that identified them idled for tens
; of thousands of cycles first, so they read the sequence and not its origin.
; Including one here would compare our arbitrary starting phase against the
; core's real one and fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the audio
; path. What that needs is its own measurement, and it is better to have a gate
; that is exact about less than one that is approximate about more.

	.=0E000

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- entry
START::	SEI
	CLD
	LDX I,0FF
	TXS

; The OUT1 latch, one bit per address, sampling D3. Zero everything: nothing
; here depends on it, but leaving it undefined would leave the two sides free
; to differ somewhere that is not the audio path.
	LDA I,00
	STA 09F00
	STA 09F01
	STA 09F02
	STA 09F03
	STA 09F04
	STA 09F05
	STA 09F06
	STA 09F07

; ------------------------------------------------------ both chips to zero
; All sixteen registers of each, so neither starts from whatever the hardware
; happened to power up holding.
	LDA I,00
	LDX I,00
ZERO:	STA AX,09800
	STA AX,09A00
	INX
	CPX I,010
	BNE ZERO

; Release init. `pokey.v:709` holds the polynomial counters in reset while both
; low bits of SKCTL are clear, which is the state from power-on; the game's
; CST.MAC:18-19 writes 7 to both chips, and so does this.
	LDA I,07
	STA 0980F
	STA 09A0F

; ------------------------------------------------------------- POKEY0
; Channel 1: pure tone. AUDC bits 7:5 = 101 selects the tone with poly5 out of
; circuit; bits 3:0 are the volume.
	LDA I,05
	STA 09800
	LDA I,0A7
	STA 09801

; Channel 2: volume-only. AUDC bit 4 emits the level continuously and ignores
; the output flip-flop, so this is a constant 3 on every sample.
	LDA I,00
	STA 09802
	LDA I,013
	STA 09803

; ------------------------------------------------------------- POKEY1
; Channel 1: a second pure tone, at a different frequency, so the two chips'
; outputs beat against each other in the sum.
	LDA I,0B
	STA 09A00
	LDA I,0A5
	STA 09A01

; ------------------------------------------------------------ synchronise
; STIMER reloads every divider and clears the output flip-flops, so both chips
; start from a defined phase rather than from wherever the register writes left
; them. Its value is immaterial; the write is the strobe.
	STA 09809
	STA 09A09

; ------------------------------------------------------------------ idle
; Nothing further touches either chip. The watchdog is strobed so the machine
; stays alive; a board that stopped being petted would reset and the sound
; would restart mid-comparison.
IDLE::	STA 09E00
	JMP IDLE

; ------------------------------------------------------------- interrupts
; Not taken — I is set at entry and never cleared — but the handler exists and
; is correct, so that a change which does enable interrupts fails loudly rather
; than wedging: acknowledge the interrupt, pet the watchdog, return.
ISR::	STA 09D80
	STA 09E00
	RTI

	.=0FFFA
	.WORD START
	.WORD START
	.WORD ISR
